{
  "refuted": false,
  "evidence": "Fetched the Wikipedia 'Processing fluency' article twice — once via WebFetch summarization and once by pulling the raw wikitext directly (curl on action=raw) and reading the full ~90-line article myself, including all Research/Applications sections and every footnoted study. The supporting quote is verbatim and in context: 'Higher fluency is often associated with more favorable evaluations, even when the ease of processing is unrelated to the content itself, a cognitive bias known as the fluency heuristic.' Every subsequent finding in the article points the same direction: fluent stimuli win on familiarity/mere-exposure (Newell & Shanks; Topolinski popcorn study), positive affect and zygomaticus activation (Reber 1998; Winkielman 2001), the illusory-truth effect for easy-to-read/rhyming/high-contrast statements (Reber 1999, Unkelbach 2007), humor ratings for easy-to-read fonts (Topolinski 2014), essay grading penalized by illegible handwriting (Greifeneder 2010), and IPO stock performance favoring pronounceable tickers (Alter & Oppenheimer 2006). The one partial counterweight is a single sentence on product design: 'While consumers enjoy a moderate source of incongruity, too much disorder and unfamiliarity lead to feelings of being overwhelmed' (citing Bloch 1995) — but this sits inside a passage whose framing sentence is that unusual/difficult-to-process product forms are 'viewed less favourably than fluent counterparts,' so the moderate-incongruity aside is a bounded caveat (an inverted-U tolerance), not a documented case of disfluency being preferred, judged more interesting, or read as more intentional/trustworthy — the three specific outcomes the claim says are absent. Nothing in the article uses the word 'trust'/'trustworthy' or frames disfluency as a signal of intentionality or authenticity at all. So the claim's characterization of the article as one-directional, with no such documented condition, is accurate, and its logical extension — that this cuts against, not for, a hypothesis that rule-breaking roughness reads as more human/trustworthy — follows directly from that one-directional content. Source quality matches claim strength: the claim is specifically about what a named, checkable secondary source (Wikipedia's article, itself citing primary peer-reviewed studies like Reber, Winkielman, Alter & Oppenheimer, Greifeneder) says, and I verified that characterization against the primary wikitext rather than trusting a paraphrase. WebSearch was unavailable this session (budget exhausted before I could run supplementary searches for contradicting disfluency-preference literature), so I could not independently canvas the wider psychology literature for a competing 'disfluency preferred' finding outside this article, but the claim under review is scoped to what the Wikipedia article establishes, and on that specific, checkable question the claim is correct.",
  "confidence": "high"
}
